WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.131
Policies target populations, never people,

2
00:00:03.131 --> 00:00:07.325
and the populations are personas,
kept identical to Book 02.

3
00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:10.912
Admins carry the most risk and
get the strictest policies.

4
00:00:10.912 --> 00:00:13.524
Internals are employees and contractors.

5
00:00:13.524 --> 00:00:17.730
Externals are partner guests, Guests
are everyone else from outside,

6
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.754
and workload identities
are service principals,

7
00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:24.738
which have to be assigned to a policy
directly, not through a group.

8
00:00:24.738 --> 00:00:27.624
And break-glass, the
two emergency accounts,

9
00:00:27.624 --> 00:00:30.350
are excluded from every enforced policy.

10
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.925
Here's the failure mode
that shapes the design.

11
00:00:32.925 --> 00:00:36.750
Persona groups are built from
attributes, so a user with an empty

12
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.863
attribute falls outside every policy
that includes only that group.

13
00:00:40.863 --> 00:00:43.137
That's an exclusion nobody approved.

14
00:00:43.137 --> 00:00:47.240
So the library includes all
users, excludes narrowly,

15
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:53.350
and adds a deliberately redundant catch-all:
MFA for all users on all resources.
